{"id": "83dd0a2c-673e-4474-ad04-73ecd30c0da1", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll map what depends on your Aurora MySQL database, so the impact assessment is based on your environment and not generic guesses. I'll also check the cluster's current setup and state. This is a read-only look and I won't open an investigation.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_LRttWBwqcdUsFL5KRgqhTd", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "aurora-impact-map", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["discovering-topology", "exploring-metrics"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Build a business-impact picture for the Aurora MySQL database(s) that back the checkout application. This is NOT a root-cause hunt; do not try to determine why checkout was slow. Only describe what depends on the database and its resilience posture.",
  "context": "A developer reported the checkout app felt slow this morning (2026-11-13) and suspects Aurora MySQL. No alarms are firing and nothing is confirmed. The user wants to know what the business impact would be IF the database had a problem. Agent space account is 012345678901_1, region us-east-1, but check other associated accounts/regions too (use list_associations).",
  "resources": ["Aurora MySQL clusters/instances", "checkout application and its compute (ECS/EKS/Lambda/EC2)", "load balancers / API Gateway in front of checkout"],
  "timeframe": "Current state, plus a quick look at the last ~12 hours of key Aurora metrics (CPUUtilization, DatabaseConnections, CommitLatency/SelectLatency, ReplicaLag, DBLoad) just to report whether anything looks abnormal. Do not go deep.",
  "criteria": "Report concisely: (1) which Aurora MySQL cluster(s) appear tied to checkout and how you know; (2) which services/apps/Lambdas/queues connect to it (upstream and downstream, including other apps sharing the same cluster, which widens the blast radius); (3) resilience config: writer/reader count, Multi-AZ, failover tiers, Aurora Serverless v2 or provisioned, instance class, backups/retention, deletion protection, read-replica use; (4) CloudWatch alarms that exist (or notable absence of alarms) on the DB and the checkout path; (5) whether the 12h metrics show anything abnormal at a glance (just yes/no with brief numbers); (6) any hints of business criticality such as tags (env, team, tier, criticality), names. Be explicit about what you could not determine."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Map Aurora MySQL dependencies and resilience for checkout impact assessment"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "8264faa0-e63c-4134-9b44-0896081aee95", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I need to flag a real contradiction here: the alarm has actually been active for 3 minutes, which conflicts with the "no alarms" claim, and the timing language ("this morning") is ambiguous without knowing the timezone. On revenue impact, using the $5,100/min figure, 20 minutes of downtime translates to roughly $102k, or about $306k per hour if sustained—though I should caveat these are tagged estimates, not confirmed figures.

Failover-wise, this is a tier-2 reader on a db.t4g.medium, so if the writer fails it'd promote to a small reader instance; I shouldn't claim an exact failover time, just hedge that Aurora typically does this in under a minute or so. I also want to note the backup retention is only 1 day, which is a real data-loss/recovery exposure, and deletion protection is off. Time to write this up.

"}]}

